<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wädenswil</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wadenswil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wädenswil is a town on the south-western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zürich</a> in the canton of Zürich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> until Zürich acquired it in the mid-sixteenth century and made it a bailiwick. In 1645–1646 its villagers took part in a revolt against new taxes, which the city suppressed with executions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wadenswil.jpg" alt="Wädenswil" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Swissbear · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ref._Kirche_W%C3%A4denswil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wädenswil lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Zürichsee</a> lakeshore in the district of Horgen in the canton of Zürich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and is locally often called Wättischwiil, Wädischwiil or Wädi. On 1 January 2019 the former municipalities of Hütten and Schönenberg were merged into it. Before that merger it covered 17.4 square kilometres, of which 59.3 per cent was used for agriculture, 9.6 per cent was forest and 29.8 per cent was settled. Most of its people speak German, with Italian and Serbo-Croatian the next most common languages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the early 1500s Wädenswil and nearby Richterswil have been home to groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, many of whom emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> in the 1700s. Many of the surnames common in the area are still found plentifully in the southeastern part of that state.</p><p>The Wädenswil brewery was a brewery from 1833 until 1990. Its building was partly demolished in 2003, but brewing in the town has continued since 1992 with the Wadi-Brau-Huus AG. The Schloss Au on the Au Peninsula, with its auxiliary buildings and park, is listed in the Swiss inventory of cultural property as an object of regional importance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wädenswil–Vorder Au is one of the 56 Swiss sites that make up the UNESCO World Heritage Site Prehistoric pile dwellings around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and the settlement is listed in the Swiss inventory of cultural property as a Class A object of national significance. The town lies on the A3 motorway and on the lakeside railway line to Zürich, and ships serve it across the lake. It is the seat of the ZHAW Wädenswil, a centre for life sciences and facility management.</p><p>Natives of the town include the landscape artist Johann Gottfried Steffan, the politician Walter Hauser, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-federal-council">Swiss Federal Council</a> from 1888 to 1902, the architect Hans Auer, who designed the Federal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the politician Robert Haab, a member of the Federal Council from 1917 to 1929, and the prehistorian Otto Hauser.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wädenswil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent sometimes erupted, as in 1489, when peasant protests and guild opposition led to the overthrow and execution of the burgomaster Hans Waldmann, and in the tax revolts of 1645–1646 around <b>Wädenswil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
